## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed from the spacing assembly of metal ions/clusters with organic ligands by coordination bonds [1,2], have received wide attention in the fields of gas adsorption and storage, chemical separations, catalysis, light harvesting, and energy storage due to their fascinating physiochemical characteristics [3-7]. Taking advantage of the structural diversity, selectable elemental species, high specific surface area, and ordered porosity, MOFs have emerged as promising precursors to prepare various functional porous carbons [8-11]. Generally, MOF-derived porous carbons can achieve designable morphology and in-situ doped heteroatoms (e.g., N, P, S, and B) by selecting appropriate MOF precursors [12,13]. The organic ligands serve as the carbon source, and the uniformly distributed metal ions show effects on crystallization and formation of porous structure [14- 17]. However, the unstable spacing coordination structure of MOFs usually causes a structure collapse during pyrolysis, resulting in severe pore shrinkage and decreased surface area [18-22]. Thus, preparation of MOF-derived porous carbons with robust skeleton and high surface area is still challenging.
To overcome the aforementioned problem, a common strategy is introducing second carbon sources in the pores of MOFs [23- 25]. For example, Xu et al. [26,27] prepared a nanocarbon with large surface area of $2872\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\m~}}^{2}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\g}}^{-1}$ and high pore volume of $2.06\ c m^{3}\ {\mathrm g}^{-1}$ by introducing furfuryl alcohol vapor into the pores of MOF-5 and following polymerization and carbonization. Zhong et al. [28] introduced carbon sources, such as xylitol, sucrose, melamine, and urea, into the pores of ZIF-8. The relevant co-derived porous carbons showed significantly improved specific surface areas and pore volumes $934{-}2159~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $0.5{-}1.4~\mathrm{cm^{3}}~\mathrm{g^{-1}},$ than the ZIF-8 derived carbon $(603~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $0.3\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ . However, the amount and distribution of additional carbon source is hard to control, and the excessive infiltration of secondary carbon precursors may result in completely blocked pores and decreased surface area of the MOF-derived carbons [29].
Constructing support framework by high-aspect-ratio graphene is another attractive strategy because these nanocarbons can simultaneously improve the conductivity of the products. Recent reports mainly focus on designing the outer support frameworks to alleviate the particle aggregation and/or to construct stable mesoporous structures. Fan et al. [30] reported a novel porous carbon with ‘‘brick-and-mortar” structure by carbonizing sandwiched MOF-5/graphene oxides hybrid materials. Graphene sheets act as the structural scaffolds for supporting MOF-5 derived porous carbon film, leading to wider pore size distribution $\left[1-8\ \mathrm{nm}\right]$ than pure MOF-5 derived carbon. Li et al. [31] prepared MOF-derived porous carbon by in-situ growth of ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 on graphene oxide and subsequent thermal treatment. The graphene serves as the scaffold to support the uniform MOF-derived carbon and achieve high specific surface area of $560\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with a broad pore size distribution ranging from 0.7 to $35\mathrm{nm}$ . Developing stable MOF precursors with inner support by rational nanomaterials may be a promising approach to prepare novel MOF-derived porous carbons with thermodynamic stability, high surface area, and tailorable pore texture [32-34]. However, using nanocarbons as the inner supports to inhibit the structure collapse and pore shrinkage of
MOFs has barely been reported before because most large-sized nanocarbons show poor compatibility with MOF precursors, which are hard to form uniformly distributed inner support on a microscale.
Herein, we develop an inner support strategy to intensify the structural stability of MOF-derived porous carbon using MOF-5 as an exemplified precursor and graphene quantum dots (GQDs) as the compatible and rigid scaffold. The small-sized GQDs have abundant carboxyl groups (–COOH), which can preferentially coordinate with metal clusters $\mathrm{[}Z\mathrm{n}_{4}0\mathrm{]}^{6+}$ , similar as the coordination between bezene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ and $\scriptstyle{[Z\ n_{4}0]^{6+}}$ in MOF-5. The uniformly introduced GQDs effectively avoid the structure collapse and pore shrinkage during pyrolysis, leading to the derived porous carbon (GMPC-0.35) possessing much higher specific surface area $(1841\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\mathrm{~.~}$ and mesopore volume $(1.62\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{.}$ ) than the conventional MOF-5 derived porous carbon (MPC, $1358\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $0.59\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ ). Moreover, the highly crystallized GQDs also construct entire conductive networks in GMPCs, contributing to an improved conductivity from 60.7 to $112.5\:\mathrm{S}\:\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ . When used as the electrode material for supercapacitors, the optimized GMPC0.35 shows an improved specific capacitance of $200\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ and remarkable rate performance with $53\%$ capacitance retention at $100\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , revealing their structure merits originated from the novel GQDs inner support.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Preparation of the GQDs
GQDs were prepared by acid oxidation method using coal powder (Heishan, Xinjiang, China) as the precursor. The details are given in our previous studies [35,36].
## 2.2. Sample preparation
MOF-5 was prepared through a solvothermal method [37]. GQD embedded MOF-5 (G-MOF-5) was synthesized by the same process with the addition of GQDs. In a typical synthesis process, $1.15\mathrm{~g~}$ $\mathtt{H}_{2}\mathtt{B D C}$ and $5.95\textrm{g Z n}(\mathrm{NO_{3}})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were dissolved into $80~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{N}$ , N-dimethylformamide (DMF). Then $_{0.40\mathrm{~g~}}$ GQDs were fully dispersed into the above mixture solution by stirring and sonication to form a homogeneous dispersion. The mixture was transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave $\mathrm{100~mL})$ and kept at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{14\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, GMOF-5 was collected by washing with DMF and methanol for several times to remove the unreacted sources, then dried at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ in vacuum overnight.
MPC and GMPC-0.35 was obtained by carbonizing MOF-5 and G-MOF-5, respectively, at $900~^{\circ}C$ for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere with the heating rate $2\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\ \mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ . After that, the obtained sample were first immersed into HCl solution $\mathrm{~\zeta~1~mol~L^{-1}~},$ ) to remove inorganic salts, and further washed to neutral using deionized water. To study the influence of GQD addition ratio on the properties of the derived carbon, GMPC-x, where ‘‘x” stands for the mass ratio of GQDs to $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ , were prepared by the same synthesis and pyrolysis process but with different GQDs dosage.
## 3. Characterization
## 3.1. Materials characterization
The morphology and structure of the samples were characterized by scanning electron microscope (SEM, Hitachi S-4800), high revolution translation electron microscopy (HRTEM, JEOL-2100F), X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 Advances), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ESCALAB 250 Xi), Raman spectroscopy (Bruker SENTERRA). The surface area and pore volume were measured by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms (ASAP 2460, Micromeritics) based on the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller method. The pore size distribution was obtained by density functional theory method [38-40].
## 3.2. Electrochemical measurements
## 4. Result and discussion
The preparation and structural transformation process of MPC and GMPC-0.35 are illustrated in Fig. 1. MOF-5 is prepared by spacing coordination of metal clusters $\mathsf{I Z n}_{4}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{I}^{6+}$ and organic ligands $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ . It shows a cubic block appearance with the size of ca. $15~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (SEM image in Figure S1a). As observed from the HRTEM image (Fig. 2a), MOF-5 shows a long-range-ordered crystal structure with a typical lattice parameter of ca. $0.28\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is indexed to the (400) crystal face. Nanosized GQDs (ca. $3.2\ \mathrm{nm}\cdot$ ) [35] show good compatibility to MOF-5 because the enriched edge carboxyl groups [42,43] can also coordinate with $\mathsf{I Z n}_{4}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{I}^{6+}$ (as seen below). Therefore, when introduced into the reaction, they play as heterogeneous nucleation centers as well as spacers, making GMOF-5 with a polycrystalline structure. As clearly demonstrated from the SEM (Figure S1b) and HRTEM image (Fig. 2b), G-MOF-5 show similar morphology and particle size with MOF-5 (the size of ca. $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}};$ ), yet they are constructed by differently oriented nanocrystals with the size from 2 to $5\mathrm{nm}$ .
From the XRD patterns (Fig. 2c), the samples all have four main diffraction peaks located at $6.8^{\circ}$ , 9.7, $13.7^{\circ}$ , and $15.4^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (2 0 0), (2 2 0), (4 0 0), and (4 2 0) crystal planes of MOF-5, respectively [44-46], indicating that GQDs are uniformly embedded in MOF-5 without disrupting the primary crystal structure. However, the (200) and (400) peaks of G-MOF-5 are more intensive than those of MOF-5. This is because the two-dimensional GQDs acting as the nucleation centers induce the preferential growth of the (200) and (400) planes, which is in well accordance with the HRTEM observed. The XPS analysis (Fig. 2d) shows that GMOF-5 have unchanged elemental compositions of $\mathsf{C}_{1s}$ , $\mathsf{N}_{1s},0_{1s},$ Zn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , and $Z\mathrm{n}~2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ located at ca. 285.0, 400.5, 531.5, 1021.3, and $1046.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, indicating GQDs are well coordinate with metal clusters $\left[Z\mathrm{n}_{4}0\right]^{6+}$ , similar as the coordination between $\mathtt{H}_{2}\mathtt{B D C}$ and $\mathrm{[}Z\mathrm{n}_{4}0\mathrm{]}^{6+}$ in MOF-5. Furthermore, the coordination state of Zn in G-MOF-5 is in well accordance with that in MOF-5 (Fig. 2e), proving that the compatible GQDs are completely incorporated into the MOF-5 scaffold. The XPS $\mathsf{C}_{1s}$ spectra (Fig. 2f) can be divided into three peaks located at around 284.6, 286.1, and $288.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ , $c{-}0$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ components, respectively. Due to the enriched $c{-}0$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ in GQDs (12 and 22 at.%) [41], G-MOF-5 possesses higher CAO (16.6 at.%) and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ contents (31.3 at.%) than MOF-5 (12.8 and $27\ \mathrm{at}.\%$ , Table S1).
After pyrolysis, MOF-5 and G-MOF-5 are converted to MPC and GMPCs, respectively. Taking the optimized GMPC-0.35 as an exemplification, the SEM images indicate that the two samples inherit the similar block appearance as the precursors (Figure S2). However, the inner structure observed from the HRTEM images are quite different. MPC shows a loose inner structure with inhomogeneous mesopores because decomposition of pure MOF-5 precursor causes the serious structure collapse and pore shrinkage (Fig. 3a). The zoomed image (Fig. 3b) shows that MPC has a typical amorphous carbon structure with abundant micropores. By contrast, the GMPC-0.35 blocks have uniformly distributed mesopores with much smaller pore size than those in MPC (Fig. 3c). Noticeably, some highly crystallized regions are uniformly distributed in the amorphous carbon matrix (Fig. 3d), which can be further confirmed from the similar chemical composition of MPC and GMPC0.35 by the XPS results (Figure S3 and Table S1), revealing the formation of rigid support by the introduced GQDs.
To reveal the inner support effect of GQDs in MOF-5 derived porous carbons, GMPCs with different mass ratio of GQDs and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ were prepared. The different porous structures and specific surface areas of the samples are presented by the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption– desorption isotherms (Fig. 4a). Obviously, the samples exhibit a combined type $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I/IV}}$ isotherms, indicating the presence of microand meso-porous structure (Fig. 4b). It is noticed that, compared with MPC, GMPCs show higher ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption volume at the low relative pressure $\left(\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}<0.05\right)$ and an obvious larger hysteresis loop at the high relative pressure regions $(\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}>0.50)$ , demonstrating a significant improvement of both total and meso- pore volumes from 0.90 and $0.59\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of MPC to 1.87 and $1.{\overset{-}{6}}2\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{3}\ {\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ of GMPC-0.35 through introducing GQDs as inner support. Therefore, GMPC-0.1 and GMPC-0.35 show much higher specific surface area of $1876\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1841~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, than MPC ( $1358\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , Fig. 4c, Table S2). As a comparison, the improved surface area of GMPC-0.35 through constructing inner support by GQDs is higher than that of reported modified porous MOF-derived carbons by outside-supported, introducing the sacrificial additional carbon sources, and other nanocarbon supports [24,28,29,47-50] (Table S3). Unfortunately, excessive GQD dosage results in the decreased surface area of GMPC-0.5 $\cdot1366\mathrm{\m}^{2}\mathrm{\bf~g}^{-1},$ . This is because the large surface area and abundant pores are mainly generated by pyrolysis of MOF-5. The specific surface area of directly pyrolyzed GQDs is only $43~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure S4).
Another interesting thing is that MPC exhibits a two-region pore size distribution with the micropores ranging from 0.7 to $1.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ and large meso- and macro-pores with the size beyond $20\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 4b). Differently, GMPCs samples contain abundant mesopores range from 2 to $20\mathrm{nm}$ instead of the two-region distribution. For pure MOF-5, decomposition of the organic ligands during pyrolysis causes serious pore collapse and shrinking, resulting in the conversion of small mesopores to micro- and macro-pores in MPC. As seen, the micropore volume of MPC $(0.31~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{\bf~g}^{-1})$ is slightly higher than those of GMPCs $\left(0.19-0.26\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{3}\thinspace\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right),$ . The uniformly embedded GQDs in G-MOF-5 precursors play as the rigid support, which significantly buffer the structure collapse of the confined MOF-5 microcrystals. Therefore, GMPC-0.35 shows the highest mesopore volume $(1.62\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}\thinspace{\mathrm{g}}^{-1})\thinspace,$ ), nearly three times higher than MPC.
Apart from the strong support function, the embedded GQDs also construct inner conductive networks in the MOF-5 derived porous carbons because of their highly crystallized feature [51].
The XRD patterns of the MPC and GMPCs exhibit weak and dispersive (002) and (10 0) diffraction peaks located at ca. $24^{\circ}$ and $43^{\circ}$ , respectively (Fig. 4d). The (002) peaks of the samples are gradually intensified with the increase of GQD dosage, indicating an improved crystallinity. The Raman spectra of MPC and GMPCs are fitted into four Gaussian peaks located at ca. 1150, 1345,
1500, and $1590~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , corresponding to the T-, D-, D’-, and Gbands, respectively (Fig. 4e and Figure S5) [40,52]. The area ratios of $\mathsf{D-}$ and G-band $(\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{D}}/\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{G}})$ monotonically decrease from 1.42 of MPC to 1.12 of GMPC-0.5, indicating the decreased defect density also owing to the highly crystallized GQDs. Therefore, the electrical conductivity greatly increases from $60.7\ \mathrm{S\m}^{-1}$ of MPC to 112.5 S $\mathfrak{m}^{-1}$ of GMPC-0.35, and $134.65\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ of GMPC-0.5 (Fig. 4f, Figure S6), which is important for electrochemical processes.
The electrochemical performance of the as-prepared samples is measured in three-electrode system. The CV curves at $10\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ and GCD profiles at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 5a, b) of the MPC and GMPCs exhibit approximate rectangular and isosceles triangular shapes, respectively, indicating their primary electric double-layer behavior. GMPCs all show larger integral CV area and longer discharge time than MPC (Figure S7 and S8), due to the inner support by GQDs, high surface area, hierarchical porous structure, and interconnected conductivity network. As a result, MPC shows capacitances of 157, 135, and $105~\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ at 1, 10, and 50 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The optimal sample GMPC-0.35 shows the highest capacitances of 200, 173, and $140\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 1, 10, and $50\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , respectively (Fig. 5c). Even at high scan rate of $200\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , the CV curves of the GMPC-0.35 still shows rectangular shape, revealing robust electrochemical kinetics. In addition, GMPC-0.35 shows a good rate performance with a high capacitance retention of $53\%$ at $100\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ .
The improved capacitive and rate performance of GMPCs is due to the novel GQD supported structure, as revealed by the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and corresponding quantitative analysis (Fig. 5d and Figure S9). The mechanism is illustrated in Fig. 5f. First, the specific surface areas of GMPCs are significantly improved by the compatible and rigid GQD support, providing abundant active sites for ion storage. Next, thanks to the improved electrical conductivity by embedding high-crystallized GQDs, the internal electrical resistance $\left(\mathsf{R}_{s}\right)$ decreases from $_{0.86\ \Omega}$ of MPC to $0.63\ \Omega$ of GMPC-0.35, and further $_{0.60\ \Omega}$ of GMPC-0.5, which facilitates efficient active sites utilization and high rate performance. Moreover, the formation of abundant mesoporous structure by GQD support enables fast ion transport inside the GMPCs. As seen from the Nyquist plots, the diameters of the semicircles in the high frequency region are gradually decreased by introducing GQDs, demonstrating the reduced charge-transfer resistance $\left(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ . The optimized GMPC-0.35 exhibits the lowest $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ value of $0.13\Omega$ and $Z_{\mathrm{w}}$ value of $0.95\Omega$ , almost two times lower than MPC $0.25\Omega$ and $2.24\Omega\backslash$ . The Warburg resistance $(Z_{\mathrm{w}})$ are also significantly decreased. Therefore, GMPC-0.35 shows the best capacitive and rate performance among the as-prepared samples. Increasing the GQD dosage can further improve the electric conductivity of $134.6\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{m}^{-1}$ for GMPC-0.5, but cause the decreased surface area and some blocked ion transport channels. Therefore, GMPC-0.5 shows increased $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}},$ $Z_{\mathrm{w}}$ values (0.14 and $1.15\ \Omega\backslash$ and the decreased capacitive and rate performance.
As another evidence, GMPC-0.5 $(1366~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ ) shows a similar specific surface area as MPC $(1358\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{.}$ ). But the capacitive performance and electrochemical kinetics of GMPC-0.5 are better than those of MPC, further proving the advantages of GQD induced mesoporous structure and entire conductive network. The improved electrochemical kinetics by the novel GQD supported structure enables a fast response of the electrode. From the Bode plots given in Fig. 5e, the relaxation time constant $\left(\tau_{0}\right)$ decreases from $2.08\ s$ of MPC to $0.94\ s$ of GMPC-0.35.
Finally, we assembled GMPC-0.35//GMPC-0.35 supercapacitor to illustrate the application performance. The rectangular shape of CV curves and isosceles triangular shapes of GCD curves (Fig. 6a and Figure S10) indicate the primary electric doublelayer behavior of GMPC-0.35 with good reversibility and fast charge–discharge capability. GMPC-0.35//GMPC-0.35 supercapacitor shows a smaller IR drop of only $0.3{\mathrm{~V~}}$ at 100 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 6b), revealing it good power ability. According to Nyquist plots and the fitted line based the equivalent circuit (inset) depicted in Figure S11, GMPC-0.35//GMPC-0.35 supercapacitor shows lower resistances $\mathbf{\delta}_{0.52}\Omega$ for $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ and $\mathbf{0.09}\Omega$ for $\begin{array}{r}{\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\dot{}.}\end{array}$ ) and better capacitive nature compared to the MPC//MPC supercapacitor $0.82\ \Omega$ for $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ and $0.21\ \Omega$ for $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\dot{}.$ . As an important index for supercapacitors, the energy and power densities are reflected in the Ragone plot (Fig. 6c). GMPC-0.35//GMPC-0.35 supercapacitor exhibits high energy densities of 7.34 and 2.50 Wh $\displaystyle{\log^{-1}}$ at the power densities of 124.57 and 5256.25 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are superior than or comparable to other MOF-5 derived carbon materials [50,53-55]. Moreover, GMPC-0.35//GMPC-0.35 supercapacitor show excellent cycle stability without capacity fading after cycling 10,000 times at $10\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 6d).
## 5. Conclusion
In summary, we have developed an inner support strategy to prepare MOF-derived porous carbon with high surface area using GQDs as compatible and rigid inner support. The uniformly embedded GQDs effective suppress the structure collapse and pore shrinkage during pyrolysis, leading to significantly improved specific surface area from 1358 to $1841~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and mesopore volume from 0.59 to $1.62~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The mesoporous structure provides abundant ion transport channels inside the GMPCs. Moreover, the highly-crystallized GQDs construct inner conductive networks, contributing to an improved conductivity from 60.7 to $112.5\mathsf{S}\mathsf{m}^{-1}$ . Owing to the above merits, the optimized GMPC-0.35 shows high specific capacitance of $200\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and good rate performance with the capacitance retention of $53\%$ at $100\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ as electrode material for supercapacitors, demonstrating the great practical application value.
